main_text:
if statements are the primary structure used to execute a block of code only if a certain condition is met (true). They skip the code inside { } if the condition is false. Note: the condition must result in a value convertible to a boolean.

Syntax:
if (condition1)
{
  // executed if condition1 is true
}
// following statements
x = x + 1;

Example:
int x;
cin >> x;
if (x < 0)
{
  x = -x;
}
cout << x << endl;

else statements are always optional and will execute when if conditions are false. This creates a mutually exclusive (one or the other) code execution structure.

Syntax:
if (condition1)
{
  // executed if condition1 is true
}
else
{
  // executed if condition1 above is false
}
// following statements

The flowchart shows condition splitting into 'If Block Statements' (True) or 'Else Block Statements' (False), both converging into 'Following statements'. Numbered annotations (1, 2, 3) map the flowchart stages to the corresponding lines of code.

Got more than 2 cases? Use 'else if'. You can have any number of else if statements; else if is optional. Reminder: else is also optional. Curly braces { } indicate the code block associated with each if, else if, or else.

Syntax:
if (condition1)
{ // executed if condition1 is true }
else if (condition2)
{ // executed if condition2 is true but condition1 was false }
else if (condition3)
{ // executed if condition3 is true but condition1 and condition2 were false }
else
{ // executed if neither condition above is true }

What will each implementation print if 'grade' is 95?

Version 1 (independent if statements, any number can execute):
if (grade >= 90) cout << "A range";
if (grade >= 80) cout << "B range";
if (grade >= 70) cout << "C range";
if (grade >= 60) cout << "D range"; else cout << "Not gonna happen!";

Version 2 (if..else if..else chain, only one block executes):
if (grade >= 90) cout << "A range";
else if (grade >= 80) cout << "B range";
else if (grade >= 70) cout << "C range";
else if (grade >= 60) cout << "D range";
else cout << "Not gonna happen!";

Using multiple ifs when if..else if..else is appropriate is not functionally wrong but is less readable and error-prone.

Guideline: If various blocks of code are mutually exclusive, put them in an if..else if..else structure rather than many individual if statements.

BAD STYLE:
if (x < 0) { cout << "negative"; }
if (x >= 0) { cout << "positive"; }

GOOD STYLE:
if (x < 0) { cout << "negative"; }
else { cout << "positive"; }

What do we put in an if or an else statement? ANYTHING! This includes expressions and variable assignment, function calls, and even other if..else statements.

Example program:
#include <iostream>
#include <cmath>
using namespace std;
int main()
{
  double val;
  cout << "Enter a pos. #" << endl;
  cin >> val;
  if( val >= 0) {
    double res = sqrt(val);
    cout << "Result=" << res << endl;
  }
  else {
    cout << "Error!" << endl;
  }
  return 0;
}

Precedence (Order of Operations): Highest = ! (NOT), Next = && (AND), Lowest = || (OR).

Better strategy: Explicitly parenthesize everything.

Ambiguous example:
if( a>0 || !(b<5) && c==0)

Explicitly parenthesized equivalent:
if( a>0 || ((!(b<5)) && c==0))

Common Mistakes:
1. Using assignment operator (=) rather than equality check operator (==). If you accidentally use '=', it converts the assigned value to a Boolean.
2. Using multiple if statements rather than if..else or if..else if statements. Without looking at the conditions, two 'if' statements imply both could be true, while 'if..else' implies one or the other.

Example:
int main()
{
  int x, y;
  cin >> x >> y;
  // Wrong!
  if( x = 0 ) { /* some code */ }
  // Right!
  if( x == 0 ) { /* some code */ }
  // Wrong!
  if(x != y) { x = 5; }
  if(x == y) { y = 7; }
  // Right
  if(x != y) { x = 5; }
  else { y = 7; }
  return 0;
}

All conditions must be formulated as a combination of comparisons of two values at a time.

Example:
int main()
{
  int x, y;
  cin >> x >> y;
  // Wrong!
  if( 0 <= x <= 9 ) { /* some code */ }
  // Right!
  if( (0 <= x) && (x <= 9) ) { /* some code */ }
  // Wrong!
  if( x == 0 || 1 ) { /* some code */ }
  // Right!
  if( (x == 0) || (x == 1) ) { /* some code */ }
  return 0;
}

A common use of a single if statement is to deal with an exceptional case. Perform a default action before the if and then use the if to correct any exceptional cases.

General structure:
// Default action
if( /* Exceptional Case */ )
{
  // Code to apply to exceptional case
}

Example 1 (shipping fee):
bool primeMember = /* set somehow */;
double shippingFee = 7.99;
if( primeMember == true )
{
  shippingFee = 0;
}

Example 2 (manager bonus):
double salary = /* some value */;
double bonus = 0.05 * salary;
if( manager == true )
{
  bonus += 1000;
}

Connection: it's often equivalent to use 'else' for one of the cases.

Rule/Exception version:
bool primeMember = /* set somehow */;
double shippingFee = 7.99;
if( primeMember == true )
{
  shippingFee = 0;
}

Equivalent if..else version:
bool primeMember = /* set somehow */;
if( primeMember == true )
{
  shippingFee = 0;
}
else
{
  shippingFee = 7.99;
}

Name: Subcase. Description: Further divide one case into one or more subcases. Structure: Nested 'if' statements.

if( /* Condition 1 */ )
{
  // Case 1 code
  if( /* Subcondition 1a */ ) {
    // Subcase 1a code
  }
  else {
    // Subcase 1b code
  }
}
else if( /* Condition 2 */ )
{
  // Case 2 code
  if( /* Subcondition 2a */ ) {
    // Subcase 2a code
  }
}

Nested if statements form a kind of AND relationship. An if..else if chain performing the same action in multiple branches could be replaced with an OR condition (e.g., classifying as Underclassmen if freshman OR sophomore).

Structural OR example (equivalent forms):
if(level=="freshman"){cout<<"Underclassmen";} else if(level=="sophomore"){cout<<"Underclassmen";} else if(level=="junior"){cout<<"Upperclassmen";} else if(level=="senior"){cout<<"Upperclassmen";}
is equivalent to: if(level=="freshman" || level=="sophomore"){cout<<"Underclassmen";}

Structural AND example (equivalent forms):
if(category=="manager" && title=="ceo"){bonus = 0.1*salary + 1000;}
is equivalent to nested ifs: if(category=="manager"){bonus=.1*salary; if(title=="ceo"){bonus+=1000;}} else {bonus=.05*salary;}

What do we put in an if condition? ANYTHING. The compiler will interpret what is in the parentheses as a Boolean: 0 = false, Non-0 = true.

Example:
int main()
{
  int x, y, val;
  bool quit;
  cin >> x >> y >> val >> quit;
  // Uses Boolean result of comparison
  if( x > 0 ) { /* code */ }
  // Uses value of bool variable. Executes if quit == true.
  if( quit ) { /* code */ }
  // Interprets number as a bool. Executes if val is non-zero
  if( val ) { /* code */ }
  // Interprets return value as bool. Executes if the min is non-zero
  if( min(x,y) ) { /* code */ }
  return 0;
}

Precedence (Order of Operations): Highest = ! (NOT), Next = && (AND), Lowest = || (OR). Better strategy: Explicitly parenthesize everything.

Ambiguous example:
bool a, b, c;
if( a && !b || c) { /* Some code */ }

Explicitly parenthesized equivalent:
bool a, b, c;
if( (a && (!b)) || c) { /* Some code */ }

Doubles effectively represent a number in binary using a technique similar to scientific notation (e.g., +7.54*10^5). However, performing operations on doubles often leads to slight rounding errors -- one result might yield +7.541*10^5 while another yields +7.539*10^5. This makes comparison difficult, so we rarely check for exact equality. Instead, we take the difference of the two numbers, take the absolute value (abs() in <cmath>), and see if it is within a small epsilon.

Example:
#include <cmath>
using namespace std;
int main()
{
  double x, y;
  x = 1.0 / 10;
  y = 0.1;
  // Wrong!
  if( x == y ) { /* some code */ }
  // Right!
  if( abs(x-y) < 1e-6 ) { /* some code */ }
  return 0;
}

C++ and the OS use the notion of streams to temporarily store (aka buffer) data to be input or output, and use the cin and cout objects (from the <iostream> library) to access those streams.

cin extracts data from the input stream [stdin] (skipping over preceding whitespace, then stopping at following whitespace).
cout inserts data into the output stream [stdout] for display by the OS.

Input example:
#include<iostream>
int main()
{
  int x;
  std::cin >> x;
  return 0;
}

Output example:
#include<iostream>
int main()
{
  std::cout << "It was the" << std::endl;
  std::cout << "best of times.";
  return 0;
}

Most C++ library components "live" in the std namespace. Think of a namespace like folders on your laptop or a classification hierarchy. So cout and endl are technically std::cout and std::endl. To avoid all that typing, we can tell the C++ compiler to look for components in the std namespace when it can't find any definition earlier in our code by writing 'using namespace std;'.

With using statement:
#include<iostream>
using namespace std;
int main()
{
  cout << "It was the" << endl;
  cout << "best of times.";
  return 0;
}

Without using statement:
#include<iostream>
int main()
{
  std::cout << "It was the" << std::endl;
  std::cout << "best of times.";
  return 0;
}

Demo: Try to compile the top program WITHOUT the using statement.

To move the cursor to the next line we need to print a new line, '\n' (char). cout only gives the characters to the OS, which then copies them to the screen. The OS may choose to delay and not print immediately, causing strange issues. endl = '\n' + a flush of the output stream.

Debugging example without endl (using '\n'):
int main() {
  task_that_might_crash(); // Doesn't crash
  cout << "Got Here 1\n";
  task_that_might_crash(); // Does crash!
  cout << "Got Here 2\n";
  return 0;
}
Output: <Segmentation fault> (no 'Got Here 1' shown because it wasn't flushed before the crash)

Debugging example with endl:
int main() {
  task_that_might_crash(); // Doesn't crash
  cout << "Got Here 1" << endl;
  task_that_might_crash(); // Does crash!
  cout << "Got Here 2" << endl;
  return 0;
}
Output: Got Here 1
<Segmentation fault>

Guideline: Use descriptive messages and endls when debugging.

Manipulators control HOW cout handles certain output options and how cin interprets the input data (but print nothing themselves). Must #include <iomanip>.

Common examples:
- setw(n): Separate consecutive outputs by n spaces
- setprecision(n): Use n digits to display doubles (both the integral + decimal parts)
- fixed: Uses the precision for only the digits after the decimal point
- boolalpha: Show Booleans as true and false rather than 1 and 0, respectively

Separated by << or >> and used inline with actual data. Other than setw, manipulators continue to apply to later output until changed.

Example:
#include <iostream>
#include <iomanip>
using namespace std;
int main()
{
  double pi = 3.14159;
  cout << pi << endl; // Prints: 3.14159
  cout << setprecision(2) << pi << endl; // Prints: 3.1
  cout << setprecision(2) << fixed << pi << endl; // Prints: 3.14
  return 0;
}

A char is just an integer type that is only 1 byte (limited range 0 to 255 or -128 to +127). cout uses the type, char or int, to infer if we want the ASCII character or integer. We can perform arithmetic/comparison operations on ASCII chars since they are converted to integers.

Example:
char c = 'a'; // same as char c = 97;
cout << c << endl; // prints 'a'
c = 97;
cout << c << endl; // prints 'a'
int x = c;
cout << x << endl; // prints 97

char d = 'a' + 1; // d now contains 98 (ASCII 'b')
cout << d << endl; // prints 'b' on the screen
if(c >= 'a' && c <= 'z') { } // && means AND, better than if(c >= 97 && c <= 122)

x = '1'
cout << x << endl; // prints: 49

The '>>' operator can be used to input any number of variables you want to read. If unexpected non-whitespace characters are encountered, cin simply stops and leaves the variable values unchanged. It does not discard the unexpected characters, so they will likely cause another error on the next read, too. More on error handling and input validation in CS103.

Example:
#include <iostream>
using namespace std;
int main()
{
  double b, h;
  cin >> b >> h;
  cout << "Area of rect: " << b * h << endl;
  cout << "Area of triangle: " << 0.5 * b * h << endl;
  return 0;
}

Given input 'xy 1.25 2', both b and h remain unset (garbage values), producing nonsensical output like 'Area of rect: 5.428E35' and 'Area of triangle: 2.714E35', since cin stopped at the non-numeric 'x' and 'y' characters and never successfully read b or h.

What do you think would happen if the user typed a double when an integer was expected? What happens if you type numeric digits when a string is expected?

Example:
#include <iostream>
#include <string>
using namespace std;
int main()
{
  int x;
  cin >> x; // User types 1.5 42
  double y, z;
  cin >> y >> z;
  string s;
  cin >> s; // User types 103.25
  cout << "x = " << x << endl;
  cout << "y,z= " << y << " " << z << endl;
  cout << "s = " << s << endl;
  return 0;
}

With input '1.5 42' then '103.25', the trace shows: x reads only '1' (stopping at the decimal point, leaving '.5' in the stream), y then reads the leftover '.5', z reads '42', and s reads the string '103.25' as text (since a string can accept digit characters).

Example program:
#include <iostream>
#include <cstdlib>
#include <ctime>
using namespace std;
int main() {
  // srand(42);
  // srand(time(0));
  cout << "RAND_MAX is" << RAND_MAX << endl;
  cout << rand() << endl;
  cout << rand() << endl;
  cout << rand() << endl;
  cout << rand() << endl;
}

(Pseudo)random number generation [(P)RNG] in C is accomplished with the rand() function declared/prototyped in cstdlib. rand() returns an integer between 0 and RAND_MAX, an integer constant defined in <cstdlib>.

Simulating a coin flip (2 outcomes, equal probability):
int r;
r = rand();
if(r < RAND_MAX/2){ cout << "Heads"; }

Generating a uniform random fraction between 0 and 1:
double r;
r = static_cast<double>(rand()) / RAND_MAX;

To reduce repetition, we can generalize our code to use a loop. Take a simple task such as outputting the first 1000 positive integers: we could write 1000 cout statements, but that would be painful. Or we could use a loop.

Without a loop (partial):
#include <iostream>
using namespace std;
int main()
{
  cout << 1 << endl;
  cout << 2 << endl;
  cout << 3 << endl;
  // hundreds more cout statements
  cout << 999 << endl;
  cout << 1000 << endl;
  return 0;
}

With a loop:
#include <iostream>
using namespace std;
int main()
{
  for(int i=1; i <= 1000; i+=1 )
  {
    cout << i << endl;
  }
  return 0;
}

Suppose we are writing a program for a simple turn-based guessing game where the user must guess a secret number. If they guess incorrectly, what should we do?

Example:
#include <iostream>
using namespace std;
int main()
{
  int guess;
  int secretNum = /* some code */
  cin >> guess;
  if(guess == secretNum) {
    cout << "You got it!" << endl;
  }
  else {
    /* What should we do here? */
  }
  return 0;
}

What if they guess wrong a second time? What should we do?

Example:
#include <iostream>
using namespace std;
int main()
{
  int guess;
  int secretNum = /* some code */
  cin >> guess;
  if(guess == secretNum) {
    cout << "You got it!" << endl;
  }
  else {
    cin >> guess;
    if(guess == secretNum) {
      cout << "You got it!" << endl;
    }
    else {
      /* What should we do here? */
    }
  }
  return 0;
}

We can never write enough if statements because someone might always use one more turn than we have if statements. But we see there is a repetitive structure in this code. Let's use a loop.

Example (three nested guesses):
#include <iostream>
using namespace std;
int main()
{
  int guess;
  int secretNum = /* some code */
  cin >> guess;
  if(guess == secretNum) {
    cout << "You got it!" << endl;
  }
  else {
    cin >> guess;
    if(guess == secretNum) {
      cout << "You got it!" << endl;
    }
    else {
      cin >> guess;
      if(guess == secretNum) {
        cout << "You got it!" << endl;
      }
      else {
        /* What should we do here? */
      }
    }
  }
  return 0;
}

There are 2 (really 2.5) kinds of loops: while (do..while) and for loops. We'll now cover how they work.

while example:
int i = 1;
while (i <= 1000)
{
  // repetitive task
  cout << i << endl;
  i++; // update
}
// following statements

for example:
int i;
for (i = 1; i <= 1000; i++)
{
  cout << i << endl;
}
// following statements

There is a variant of the while loop which is the do..while loop, covered later. 4 parts: Initialization, Condition, Body, Update.

Loops involve writing a task to be repeated. Regardless of that task, there must be 4 parts to make a loop work:
- Initialization: Initialization of the variable(s) that will control how many iterations the loop will execute. Executed only once!
- Condition: Condition to decide whether to repeat the task or stop the loop.
- Body: Code to repeat for each iteration.
- Update: Modify the variable(s) related to the condition (without the update, the condition could be TRUE forever, leading to an 'infinite loop').

for(int i=1; i <= 1000; i+=1 )
{
  cout << i << endl;
}
// Code after

int i=1;
while(i <= 1000) {
  cout << i << endl;
  i++;
}
// Code After

A while loop is essentially a repeating 'if' statement.

if version:
// initialization
if (condition)
{
  // executed if condition1 is true
}
// following statements

while version:
// initialization
while (condition)
{
  // executed if condition1 is true
  // update statement
} // go to top, eval cond1 again
// following statements

The major change between if and while is that after executing the block, while loops back to re-evaluate the condition, rather than proceeding to the following statements.

A while loop is essentially a repeating 'if' statement.

initialization
while (condition1)
{
  // Body: if condition1 is true
  // Update statement
} // go to top, eval cond1 again
// following statements
// only gets here when cond1 is false

The numbered trace (steps 1-9, evaluating condition as True, True, False across iterations) walks through the flowchart boxes: Initialization (e.g. i=1) -> Condition (e.g. i <= 1000) -> Loop task (e.g. cout << i << endl) -> Update Statement (e.g. i += 1) -> back to Condition -> ... -> Code after the loop once condition is False.

What do we put in a while or for loop body? ANYTHING! This includes expressions and variable assignment, function calls, if..else statements, and even other loops.

Example:
#include <iostream>
using namespace std;
int main()
{
  int guess;
  int secretNum = /* some code */
  cin >> guess;
  while(guess != secretNum)
  {
    cout << "Enter guess: " << endl;
    cin >> guess;
  }
  cout << "You got it!" << endl;
  return 0;
}

Trace through the code and show all changes to y for: y = 18 and then y = 6.

int main()
{
  int y;
  cin >> y;
  while(y > 0){
    if( y >= 10 ){
      y -= 5;
    }
    else if( y >= 5 ){
      y -= 3;
    }
    else {
      y -= 1;
    }
    cout << y << endl;
  }
  return 0;
}

Ensure you understand the meaning (semantics) of a while loop by tracing through the code to the right. Show all changes to x for: x = 24 and then x = 27.

int main()
{
  int x, y;
  cin >> x;
  while( (x % 2) == 0){
    x = x/2;
  }
  return 0;
}

'for' loops have the same ability as a 'while' loop but make the 4 parts of a loop EXPLICIT.

while version:
// initialization
while (condition)
{
  // executed if condition is true
  // Update statement
}
// following statements

for version:
for( init; condition; update)
{
  // executed if condition is true
} // go to top, do update, eval cond. again
// following statements

Example:
for( int i=1; i < 1000; i++)
{
  cout << i << endl;
}

'for' loop performs the initialization statement once; checks the condition each iteration before deciding to execute the body or end the loop; performs the update statement after each execution of the body.

for( init; condition; update)
{
  // executed if condition is true
} // go to top, do update, eval cond. again
// following statements
// only gets here when cond. is false

The numbered trace (steps 1-9, True/True/False) shows the execution order: init runs once (step 1), then condition, body, update repeat until the condition is false.

Example 1:
#include <iostream>
using namespace std;
int main()
{
  int i;
  for(i=0; i < 5; i++)
  {
    cout << i << endl;
  }
  return 0;
}
Program Output: 0 1 2 3 4

Example 2:
#include <iostream>
using namespace std;
int main()
{
  int i;
  for(i=8; i > 0; i=i/2 )
  {
    cout << i << endl;
  }
  return 0;
}
Program Output: 8 4 2 1

The initial value, condition, and update statement can be any valid expression!

A tangent that will be relevant in our discussion of for loops is the idea of scope. Scope refers to the lifetime and visibility of a variable. Recall variables are just memory slots in the computer; the program will reclaim those memory spots when a variable 'dies'. In C/C++, a variable's scope is the curly braces {} it is declared within. Main Point: A variable dies at the end of the {...} it was declared in.

Example:
#include <iostream>
using namespace std;
int main()
{
  int i;
  cin >> i;
  if(i > 0){
    int temp = 2*i;
    cout << temp << endl;
  } // temp died here
  temp = i++; // won't compile
  cout << temp << endl;
  return 0;
} // i dies here

void f1()
{
  // is i visible here?
  cout << i << endl;
}

The initialization statement can be used to declare a control/inductive variable, but its scope is considered to be the for loop (even though it is not technically declared in the {..} of the for loop). Just realize that variable will die at the end of the loop. However, because it dies after the first loop, you can use that same variable name in a subsequent loop.

Example:
#include <iostream>
using namespace std;
int main()
{
  int n;
  cin >> n;
  for(int i=0; i < n; i++){
    cout << 3*i << endl;
  } // i dies here
  // won't compile
  cout << i << endl;
  // okay to reuse i
  for(int i=0; i < n; i++){
    cout << 4*i << endl;
  } // reincarnated i dies again
  return 0;
} // n dies here

'Scope' of a variable refers to the visibility (who can access it) and lifetime of a variable (how long the memory is reserved). For now, there are 2 scopes we will learn:
- Global: Variables declared outside of any function, visible to all code/functions in the program. For various reasons, it is 'bad' practice to use global variables. You MAY NOT use them in CS 102.
- Local: Variables declared inside of a function, only visible in that function, and die when the function ends.

Example:
#include <iostream>
using namespace std;
// Global Variable
int x=1;
int add_x()
{
  int n; // n is a "local" variable
  cin >> n;
  // y and z NOT visible (in scope) here
  // but x is since it is global
  return (n + x);
} // n dies here
int main()
{
  // y and z are "local" variables
  int y=0, z;
  z = add_x();
  y += z / x; // n is NOT visible
  cout << x << " " << y << endl;
  return 0;
} // y and z die here

